From 788a6fa3f4dd235785196d2612b18aef0e2e296f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Wed, 12 Aug 2026 07:57:09 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 08883/11362] SYSTEMIC: 14 of 21 stored goal predicates never
 fire on a real win; 4 planner solves are hollow

REQ-ARC-WMTE-6257, CPU-only sweep of every engine in the tracked store. All 14
score a PERFECT 1.0 on score_goal_predicate_consistency, because held-out data
contains no level-ups and a constant-False predicate is 100% correct against it.
Only the sensitivity side -- does it fire on a grid where a level-up really
happened -- can fail them. Five discriminate (cd82 cn04 r11l sk48 sp80), three
fire on everything, four are unmeasurable for lack of a banked win grid.

THE PLANNING CONSEQUENCE, measured not inferred. plan_in_model terminates on
is_level_complete(next_grid). Cross-referencing this sweep against exp6252's runs
over the same 25 games: 10 of the 14 degenerate games found NO plan at the 20000
node cap -- the termination condition can never be true. Four found a plan anyway,
which looked like a contradiction, so it was checked instead of explained away.
Replaying each plan inside its own induced model and testing the predicate on the
terminal grid: ls20, m0r0, s5i5 and vc33 ALL fire on the in-model terminal grid
and ALL fail on the real win grid. Those four plans are hollow -- the planner
reached a state the model calls a win and reality does not.

THIS QUALIFIES exp6252's NEGATIVE. That experiment asked whether a goal gradient
improves planning and answered no. Most of its roster turns out to have had a
termination condition that was unreachable (10 games) or wrong (4). Heap ordering
cannot rescue a search that cannot terminate correctly, so the null was
over-determined. The verdict stands; the interpretation "gradients do not help
planning" is much weaker than it looked.

WHY IT MATTERS. Everything else tested this session -- search structure, sampling
breadth, goal gradient, model architecture, win exemplar -- probed the DYNAMICS
half of induction and came back null or negative. This is a defect in the GOAL
half: systemic across two thirds of the store, invisible to the project's own
goal-consistency metric, and it directly breaks the planner's termination
condition.

arc_llm_reinduction already carries degenerate_goal_predicate rejection, so the
failure mode is known. What is new is that the CONSISTENCY score rates these
predicates perfect, so the two mechanisms are not interchangeable and anything
gating on the former alone admits constant-False predicates.

No fix attempted. Candidates in cost order: add a sensitivity term to
score_goal_predicate_consistency whenever a win grid exists; re-induce the 14;
check whether the LIVE path's runtime-induced predicates share the defect.

